! HELPLIB.HLP: DCL's help, which build.rs puts in SYS$HELP:HELPLIB.HLB.
! A topic for each verb in vms/cld/*.cld and each DCL does itself, and a
! few on how DCL reads a command. Written for vaxpunk, from what its DCL
! and programs do: describe only what they take.
1 @
  Runs a command procedure: a file of DCL commands, each line starting
  with a dollar sign.

  Format

    @file-spec [p1 [p2 [... p8]]]

  The file's type is .COM unless you give one. The procedure runs one
  level deeper than the command that called it; its parameters become
  the local symbols P1 to P8, and those you leave out are empty
  strings. The lines after a command that runs an image, up to the next
  line that starts with $, are that image's input.

  An error or a fatal error ends the procedure, unless ON or SET NOON
  says otherwise, and the @ command takes its status. EXIT ends it
  early. SYS$MANAGER:SYLOGIN.COM and your SYS$LOGIN:LOGIN.COM run this
  way when you log in.
2 Parameters

 file-spec

  The command procedure to run.

 p1 ... p8

  Up to eight parameters, separated by blanks. Put one in quotes to keep
  its blanks and its lowercase letters.
2 Examples

  $ @DCLTEST 3 "Two words"

  Runs DCLTEST.COM with P1 "3" and P2 "Two words".
1 =
  Gives a symbol a value: an integer or a string.

  Format

    symbol-name = expression
    symbol-name == expression

  One equals sign makes a local symbol, the procedure's that made it,
  which the procedures it calls see too. Two make a global symbol,
  which every procedure sees. The expression is worked out first; see
  the topic Expressions.
2 Examples

  $ X = 6 * 7
  $ GREETING == "Hello, " + F$TRNLNM("SYS$NODE")
1 :=
  Gives a symbol the rest of the line as a string, without working it
  out as an expression.

  Format

    symbol-name := string
    symbol-name :== string

  The string is made capitals and its blanks compressed, but where it is
  in quotes. := makes a local symbol, :== a global one.

  A string that is a dollar sign and an image's name makes the symbol a
  foreign command: typing the symbol and some arguments runs the image,
  SYS$SYSTEM:image.EXE unless you say where, and the image reads the
  arguments with LIB$GET_FOREIGN.
2 Examples

  $ HOME :== SET DEFAULT SYS$LOGIN
  $ HOME

  $ DUMP :== $SYS$SYSTEM:RMSDUMP
  $ DUMP MYFILE.DAT
1 ANALYZE
  Analyzes the structure of RMS files.

  Format

    ANALYZE/RMS_FILE file-spec[,...]
2 /RMS_FILE
  Reports on each file's RMS structure: its header, attributes and,
  for a relative or indexed file, its prologue, areas and keys. The
  report goes in pages under a heading, on SYS$OUTPUT unless /OUTPUT
  names a file.

  The status says how it went: all well, errors found, or the file so
  damaged the check could not go on.
3 Parameters

 file-spec[,...]

  The files to analyze, separated by commas.
3 Qualifiers
/CHECK
  /CHECK

  Walks each key's index and data buckets and reports what is wrong
  with them. This is the default.
/FDL
  /FDL

  Writes an FDL file that describes the file, instead of a report, to
  /OUTPUT's file, or to file-name.FDL. CREATE/FDL or CONVERT/FDL can
  make the file again from it.
/OUTPUT
  /OUTPUT[=file-spec]

  Writes the report, or the FDL file, there instead.
/STATISTICS
  /STATISTICS

  Reports each area's and key's figures as well as checking.
/SUMMARY
  /SUMMARY

  Reports the file's attributes and keys without walking its buckets.
1 APPEND
  Adds the records of files to the end of another.

  Format

    APPEND input-file-spec[,...] output-file-spec
2 Parameters

 input-file-spec[,...]

  The files whose records are added: a list, each with wildcards or not.

 output-file-spec

  The file they go at the end of. It must exist.
2 Qualifiers
/CONFIRM
  /CONFIRM
  /NOCONFIRM (default)

  Asks before appending each file. YES, TRUE or 1 appends it; NO,
  FALSE, 0 or Return doesn't; ALL appends it and the rest without
  asking; QUIT or CTRL/Z stops.
/LOG
  /LOG
  /NOLOG (default)

  Says which file was appended to which.
1 BACKUP
  Saves files in a save set, a single file in BACKUP's format, or
  restores or lists the files of one. VMS's BACKUP can read the save
  sets it writes.

  Format

    BACKUP input-spec output-save-set/SAVE_SET
    BACKUP input-save-set/SAVE_SET output-directory
    BACKUP/LIST input-save-set/SAVE_SET
2 Parameters

 input-spec

  The files to save, or the save set to read. Wildcards are allowed in
  the files to save; what the specification leaves out is *.*;*.

 output-spec

  The save set to write, or the directory to restore the files into.
  With /LIST you may leave it out, and the save set is only listed.
2 Qualifiers
/LIST
  /LIST

  Lists the save set's files as it reads it.
/LOG
  /LOG

  Says what each file saved or restored was.
/SAVE_SET
  /SAVE_SET

  Put after the parameter that is the save set: the output when saving,
  the input when restoring or listing.
2 Examples

  $ BACKUP [SYSMGR]*.COM DKB0:[000000]MGR.BCK/SAVE_SET
  $ BACKUP/LIST DKB0:[000000]MGR.BCK/SAVE_SET
  $ BACKUP DKB0:[000000]MGR.BCK/SAVE_SET DKB0:[RESTORED]
1 CLOSE
  Closes a file OPEN opened.

  Format

    CLOSE logical-name
2 Parameters

 logical-name

  The name OPEN gave the file.
2 Qualifiers
/ERROR
  /ERROR=label

  Goes to the label in the command procedure if the file can't be
  closed, or wasn't opened.
/LOG
  /LOG (default)
  /NOLOG

  Says so when the name isn't of a file OPEN opened. /NOLOG says
  nothing.
1 CONNECT
  Connects your terminal's line to a virtual terminal of yours that is
  disconnected, VTAn:, whose process then goes on there. SHOW
  USERS/FULL shows which ones are disconnected.

  Format

    CONNECT virtual-terminal-name

  Your own terminal must be a virtual one too: a TELNET login has one
  while the system logical name TCPIP$TELNET_VTA is TRUE. LOGINOUT
  offers the same when you log in and have a disconnected process.
2 Parameters

 virtual-terminal-name

  The virtual terminal to connect to, VTAn:.
2 Qualifiers
/CONTINUE
  /CONTINUE
  /NOCONTINUE (default)

  Continues the image CTRL/Y stopped in this process, which keeps
  running, disconnected, as with /NOLOGOUT.
/LOGOUT
  /LOGOUT (default)
  /NOLOGOUT

  Logs this process out once its line is connected to the other.
  /NOLOGOUT keeps it, disconnected, for a CONNECT back to it later.
2 Example

  $ SHOW USERS/FULL
  $ CONNECT VTA1:

  Connects to the process on VTA1:, which was disconnected when its
  connection closed, and logs this one out.
1 CONTINUE
  Goes on with the image CTRL/Y stopped, from where it stopped.

  Format

    CONTINUE

  A command that runs another image ends the stopped one instead.
1 CONVERT
  Copies the records of one or more files into a new file, which can
  have another organization, other record attributes or other keys.

  Format

    CONVERT input-file-spec[,...] output-file-spec

  The output file is made as /FDL's file describes it, or like the
  first input file. An indexed output gets the records in the order of
  its primary key. A record the output can't take is an exception, and
  CONVERT says why.
2 Parameters

 input-file-spec[,...]

  The files whose records are copied, in order, separated by commas.

 output-file-spec

  The file made.
2 Qualifiers
/APPEND
  /APPEND

  Adds the records to an existing sequential output file.
/EXCEPTIONS_FILE
  /EXCEPTIONS_FILE[=file-spec]

  Writes the records the output can't take to that file.
/FDL
  /FDL=fdl-file-spec

  Makes the output file as the FDL file describes it.
/KEY
  /KEY=n

  Reads an indexed input along key n, 0 the primary key.
/MERGE
  /MERGE

  Adds the records to an existing indexed output file, each where its
  key puts it.
/PAD
  /PAD[=character]

  Pads a record too short for a fixed-length output, with zeros or the
  character given.
/SORT
  /SORT (default)
  /NOSORT

  Sorts the records by the output's primary key before loading an
  indexed file. /NOSORT puts them in as they come.
/STATISTICS
  /STATISTICS

  Reports how many records were read and written, and how long it took.
/TRUNCATE
  /TRUNCATE

  Cuts a record too long for the output to the size it takes.
2 Examples

  $ CONVERT/FDL=CUSTOMERS.FDL CUSTOMERS.TXT CUSTOMERS.DAT
1 COPY
  Copies files. COPY/HTTP fetches one from a web server.

  Format

    COPY input-file-spec[,...] output-file-spec
    COPY/HTTP node::"/path" output-file-spec
    COPY/HTTP URL::"http://host[:port]/path" output-file-spec

  The new file has the input's organization, record attributes and
  size. What the output specification leaves out comes from the
  input's: COPY A.TXT [.X]*.* makes [.X]A.TXT. The new file is the next
  version unless the output gives one. A relative or indexed file is
  copied whole, its structure and all.
2 Parameters

 input-file-spec[,...]

  The files to copy: a list, by commas or plus signs, each with
  wildcards or not. One that leaves out its device and directory takes
  those of the one before.

 output-file-spec

  The file to make.
2 Qualifiers
/ALLOCATION
  /ALLOCATION=number-of-blocks

  Makes each new file that many blocks to start with, rather than the
  input's size.
/BINARY
  /BINARY

  With /HTTP, makes the file fixed-length records of 512 bytes, rather
  than a stream file.
/CONCATENATE
  /CONCATENATE (default)
  /NOCONCATENATE

  Makes one file of all the inputs, where the output gives its name
  and type: the first copied, the others appended. An output that
  leaves out its name or type, or /NOCONCATENATE, makes a file for each.
/CONFIRM
  /CONFIRM
  /NOCONFIRM (default)

  Asks before copying each file. YES, TRUE or 1 copies it; NO, FALSE,
  0 or Return doesn't; ALL copies it and the rest without asking; QUIT
  or CTRL/Z stops.
/HTTP
  /HTTP

  Fetches the input from a web server with an HTTP GET. The node is the
  server's address or name, and the path in quotes is the page; or the
  quoted string is a whole URL, http:// or https://, and the node any
  name, URL by custom. https:// goes through TLS and checks the
  server's certificate. The file is named after the path's last part
  where the output leaves its name out. Redirects are followed.
/LOG
  /LOG
  /NOLOG (default)

  Says what was copied where, and how many records, and which file was
  replaced or overwritten.
/OVERLAY
  /OVERLAY
  /NOOVERLAY (default)

  Writes the records into the output file, if it is there, in place of
  its own, rather than making a new version. The file keeps its
  attributes. Only a sequential file can be overwritten.
/PROTECTION
  /PROTECTION=(category[:access][,...])

  Gives the new file's categories, SYSTEM, OWNER, GROUP or WORLD, that
  access, R, W, E and D. The ones it doesn't name keep the protection
  the file would have had.
/REPLACE
  /REPLACE
  /NOREPLACE (default)

  Deletes the version the output names, if it is there, and makes the
  file in its place. Without it, copying to a version that is there
  fails.
2 Examples

  $ COPY/LOG SYS$SYSDEVICE:[SYSMGR]WELCOME.TXT RAM.TXT

  $ COPY *.TXT ALL.TXT

  $ COPY/REPLACE/PROTECTION=(W:R) A.TXT B.TXT;1

  $ COPY/HTTP URL::"https://example.com/" []
1 CREATE
  Makes a file of the lines you type, a directory, an empty file that
  an FDL file describes, a mailbox, or a logical name table.

  Format

    CREATE file-spec
    CREATE/DIRECTORY directory-spec
    CREATE/FDL=fdl-file-spec [file-spec]
    CREATE/MAILBOX logical-name
    CREATE/NAME_TABLE table-name

  Without a qualifier CREATE reads lines from SYS$INPUT up to CTRL/Z
  and writes them to a new version of the file, as text.
2 Parameters

 file-spec

  The file or directory to make. With /FDL it may be left out, and the
  FDL's FILE NAME names it.
2 Qualifiers
/DIRECTORY
  /DIRECTORY

  Makes the directory, [dir] or [dir.sub...], and those above it that
  aren't there.
/FDL
  /FDL=fdl-file-spec

  Makes an empty file as the FDL file describes it: its organization,
  record attributes, areas and keys.
/LOG
  /LOG

  Says the file was created.
2 Examples

  $ CREATE/DIRECTORY DKB0:[PROJECT.SOURCES]
  $ CREATE/FDL=CUSTOMERS.FDL
2 /MAILBOX
  Makes a mailbox, MBnn:, that the logical name stands for, so that
  processes can send each other messages through it. A temporary
  mailbox, the default, takes TMPMBX and CMEXEC and goes when your
  process does; a permanent one, /PERMANENT, takes PRMMBX and stays.
  The logical name goes in the system table, which takes SYSNAM.

  Format

    CREATE/MAILBOX logical-name
3 Qualifiers
/BUFFER_SIZE
  /BUFFER_SIZE=n

  The bytes of messages the mailbox holds, 1056 by default.
/LOG
  /LOG

  Says the mailbox was created, and its name.
/MESSAGE_SIZE
  /MESSAGE_SIZE=n

  The longest message, up to 65535 bytes, 256 by default.
/PERMANENT
  /PERMANENT

  Makes a permanent mailbox.
/TEMPORARY
  /TEMPORARY (default)

  Makes a temporary mailbox.
3 Example

  $ CREATE/MAILBOX/PERMANENT/MESSAGE_SIZE=512/LOG MY_MAILBOX
  %CREATE-I-CREATED, MB12: created
2 /NAME_TABLE
  Makes a logical name table, in the process's directory,
  LNM$PROCESS_DIRECTORY, so that its names are the process's. Under one
  of the system's tables it goes in LNM$SYSTEM_DIRECTORY, for every
  process, which takes write access to that directory. A table by that
  name stays, and DCL says so, unless /ATTRIBUTES=SUPERSEDE.

  Format

    CREATE/NAME_TABLE table-name
3 Qualifiers
/ATTRIBUTES
  /ATTRIBUTES=(keyword[,...])

  SUPERSEDE replaces a table by that name, and the tables under it.
  CONFINE and NO_ALIAS are taken, and have no effect yet.
/LOG
  /LOG (default)
  /NOLOG

  Says when the table was there already, or was replaced.
/PARENT_TABLE
  /PARENT_TABLE=table-name

  The table it goes under, LNM$PROCESS_DIRECTORY by default.
3 Example

  $ CREATE/NAME_TABLE MYTAB
  $ DEFINE/TABLE=MYTAB FOO BAR
  $ DEFINE/TABLE=LNM$PROCESS_DIRECTORY LNM$FILE_DEV LNM$PROCESS,MYTAB,LNM$SYSTEM
1 DEASSIGN
  Deletes a logical name.

  Format

    DEASSIGN logical-name
2 Parameters

 logical-name

  The logical name to delete.
2 Qualifiers
/PROCESS
  /PROCESS (default)

  Deletes it from the process's table, LNM$PROCESS.
/SYSTEM
  /SYSTEM

  Deletes it from the system's table, LNM$SYSTEM. You need SYSNAM.
/TABLE
  /TABLE=table-name

  Deletes it from the first table that table-name stands for that has
  it. A table's name in LNM$PROCESS_DIRECTORY or LNM$SYSTEM_DIRECTORY
  deletes the table.
1 DECK
  Marks the start of data lines in a command procedure that may start
  with $, for the command or program before it to read.

  Format

    DECK

  Without DECK a line that starts with $ ends the data. After it, only
  EOD does, or with /DOLLARS=string a line that starts with string. Each
  ends one set of data; the next ends at a $ again.
2 Qualifiers
/DOLLARS
  /DOLLARS[=string]

  Ends the data at a line that starts with string, 1 to 15 characters,
  in capitals unless in quotes. Use it when the data has $ EOD lines.
  Without a string, EOD ends it.
2 Examples

  $ CREATE WEATHER.COM
  $ DECK
  $ WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "Sunny"
  $ EOD
  $ @WEATHER
1 DEFINE
  Makes a logical name, which stands for its equivalence name wherever
  a file or device is named.

  Format

    DEFINE logical-name equivalence-name[,...]

  More than one equivalence name makes a search list: a file is looked
  for in each in turn.
2 Parameters

 logical-name

  The name to define.

 equivalence-name[,...]

  What it stands for: a device, a directory, a file, or another logical
  name.
2 Qualifiers
/LOG
  /LOG (default)
  /NOLOG

  Says so when the name replaces one already defined.
/PROCESS
  /PROCESS (default)

  Puts the name in the process's table, LNM$PROCESS.
/SYSTEM
  /SYSTEM

  Puts it in the system's table, LNM$SYSTEM, for every process. You
  need SYSNAM.
/TABLE
  /TABLE=table-name

  Puts it in that table, or the first one table-name stands for: a name
  in LNM$PROCESS_DIRECTORY or LNM$SYSTEM_DIRECTORY, such as LNM$FILE_DEV,
  may stand for a list of tables.
2 Examples

  $ DEFINE HOME MDA0:[000000],SYS$MANAGER
  $ DIRECTORY HOME:
1 DELETE
  Deletes files. DELETE/SYMBOL deletes symbols.

  Format

    DELETE file-spec
    DELETE/SYMBOL symbol-name
    DELETE/SYMBOL/ALL
2 Parameters

 file-spec

  The files to delete. It must give a version number, or ;* for every
  version. Wildcards are allowed.
2 Qualifiers
/LOG
  /LOG
  /NOLOG (default)

  Says which files were deleted.
2 /SYMBOL
  Deletes a symbol: the local one, unless /GLOBAL says otherwise.

    DELETE/SYMBOL symbol-name
    DELETE/SYMBOL/ALL
3 Qualifiers
/ALL
  /ALL

  Deletes every symbol of the level, in place of a name: the local
  ones, or with /GLOBAL the global ones.
/GLOBAL
  /GLOBAL

  Deletes the global symbol.
/LOCAL
  /LOCAL (default)

  Deletes the local symbol.
2 Examples

  $ DELETE/LOG RAM.TXT;1
  $ DELETE *.TMP;*
  $ DELETE/SYMBOL/GLOBAL HOME
1 DIRECTORY
  Lists files.

  Format

    DIRECTORY [file-spec[,...]]

  It lists each directory's files under a heading, four names to a
  line, then how many there are. A search list, or a wildcard
  directory, is several directories, and a grand total ends the list.
2 Parameters

 file-spec

  The files to list. What it leaves out is *.*;* in the default
  directory. * matches any characters and % one; [*], [dir...] and
  [...] are wildcard directories. In a list, by commas, each one
  takes what it leaves out from the one before: DIRECTORY [X]A.TXT,B
  lists [X]A.TXT and [X]B.TXT. One that can't be read is an error,
  and the list goes on.
2 Qualifiers
/BRIEF
  /BRIEF (default)

  Lists only the names. /SIZE, /DATE, /OWNER, /PROTECTION and /FULL
  override it.
/COLUMNS
  /COLUMNS=n

  Lists n names to a line, 4 if it doesn't say, as many as fit in 80
  characters.
/DATE
  /DATE[=(keyword[,...])]

  Lists a file a line, with its dates: CREATED (the default), MODIFIED,
  EXPIRED, BACKUP, EFFECTIVE, RECORDING, ACCESSED, ATTRIBUTES,
  DATA_MODIFIED, or ALL of them.
/FULL
  /FULL

  Lists everything about each file: its size, owner, protection, file
  organization, record attributes and the rest.
/OWNER
  /OWNER

  Lists a file a line, with its owner's UIC.
/PROTECTION
  /PROTECTION

  Lists a file a line, with its protection.
/SIZE
  /SIZE[=keyword]

  Lists a file a line, with the blocks it uses, USED (the default), it
  has, ALLOCATION, or ALL, both; the totals add them up.
/TOTAL
  /TOTAL

  Lists only each directory's heading and total.
/VERSIONS
  /VERSIONS=n

  Lists only the n highest versions of each file.
2 Examples

  $ DIRECTORY
  $ DIRECTORY/OWNER/PROTECTION SYS$SYSTEM:*.EXE
  $ DIRECTORY/SIZE=ALL/DATE/VERSIONS=1
  $ DIRECTORY DKB0:[W...]*.TMP
1 DISCONNECT
  Disconnects your virtual terminal from its line, which closes: the
  process stays, and waits for its terminal, until you log in again
  and connect to it, or for TTY_TIMEOUT, 15 minutes, after which it is
  logged out.

  Format

    DISCONNECT

  Only a virtual terminal, VTAn:, can be disconnected.
2 Qualifiers
/CONTINUE
  /CONTINUE
  /NOCONTINUE (default)

  Continues the image CTRL/Y stopped, which runs on while the terminal
  is disconnected, until it reads or writes there.
1 DISMOUNT
  Dismounts a volume. Its files can't be named again until it is
  mounted. A volume mounted /SYSTEM requires SYSNAM; a private one, a
  process of the job that mounted it.

  Format

    DISMOUNT device-name
2 Parameters

 device-name

  The device whose volume is dismounted, as DKB0:.
1 EDIT
  Edits a file. EDIT starts EVE, the editor of the Text Processing
  Utility, TPU; EDIT/EDT starts EDT.

  Format

    EDIT [file-spec]
    EDIT/EDT file-spec

  In EVE, press Help or type HELP after Do for its commands.
2 Parameters

 file-spec

  The file to edit. EVE makes it if it isn't there.
2 Qualifiers
/COMMAND
  /COMMAND=file-spec

  The TPU command file to run after the section, TPU$COMMAND.TPU by
  default.
/DISPLAY
  /DISPLAY (default)
  /NODISPLAY

  /NODISPLAY runs TPU without the screen, for a command file that
  edits on its own.
/EDT
  /EDT

  Starts EDT, line mode first: type CHANGE for keypad mode, and HELP for
  its commands.
/SECTION
  /SECTION=file-spec

  The section file TPU loads, SYS$SHARE:EVE$SECTION by default: a
  .TPU$SECTION file, or TPU source compiled as it starts.
/TPU
  /TPU (default)

  Starts TPU and EVE.
2 Examples

  $ EDIT LOGIN.COM
  $ EDIT/EDT NOTES.TXT
1 ELSE
  Starts the commands a block IF does when its expression is false.
  See IF.
1 ENDIF
  Ends a block IF. See IF.
1 EOD
  Ends the data lines DECK started, or any data lines, in a command
  procedure. The command or program reading them sees the end of the
  file there. See DECK.

  Format

    EOD
1 EXIT
  Ends the command procedure, going back to the one that called it.

  Format

    EXIT [status]

  The status, the value of $STATUS if you leave it out, becomes the
  status of the @ command. Typed after CTRL/Y, outside a procedure,
  EXIT makes the stopped image exit, through its exit handlers.
2 Parameters

 status

  An expression, the status to return.
1 Expressions
  DCL works out an expression wherever a command takes one: after = and
  ==, IF and WRITE, and in a lexical function's arguments.

  Its values are integers and strings. An operand is a number,
  "a string" in quotes, a symbol's name or a lexical function. A number
  can be decimal, %X hexadecimal, %O octal or %D decimal.

  The operators, from the first done to the last:

    -  .NOT.       before an operand
    *  /           multiply, divide
    +  -           add, subtract; + joins two strings
    .EQ. .NE. .LT. .LE. .GT. .GE.
                   compare integers
    .EQS. .NES. .LTS. .LES. .GTS. .GES.
                   compare strings
    .AND.          and, bit by bit
    .OR.           or, bit by bit

  Parentheses change the order. Where an integer is needed a string
  is its number, or 1 if it starts with T or Y, else 0. A comparison is
  1 when true, 0 when false; IF takes an odd value as true.
2 Substitution
  Before a command is done, 'name' becomes the value of symbol name. In
  quotes, write ''name' instead. An exclamation mark outside quotes
  starts a comment, which DCL drops.
1 FORMS
  The DECforms development commands. TRANSLATE makes a form file of an
  IFDL source; BACK_TRANSLATE makes IFDL of a form file.

  Format

    FORMS TRANSLATE ifdl-file-spec
    FORMS BACK_TRANSLATE form-file-spec
2 TRANSLATE
  Translates an IFDL source, file.IFDL, into a binary form file,
  file.FORM.
3 Qualifiers
/COMMENTS
  /COMMENTS (default)
  /NOCOMMENTS

  Keeps the source's comments in the form, for BACK_TRANSLATE.
/ERROR_LIMIT
  /ERROR_LIMIT=n

  Stops after n errors.
/LIST
  /LIST[=file-spec]

  Writes a listing of the source and its messages.
/LOG
  /LOG

  Says what file was made.
/OUTPUT
  /OUTPUT=file-spec (default)
  /NOOUTPUT

  Names the form file, or with /NOOUTPUT only checks the source.
/WARNINGS
  /WARNINGS (default)
  /NOWARNINGS

  /NOWARNINGS leaves out the warnings.
2 BACK_TRANSLATE
  Makes the IFDL source of a form file, file.IFDL.
3 Qualifiers
/LOG
  /LOG

  Says what file was made.
/OUTPUT
  /OUTPUT=file-spec

  Names the IFDL file.
1 GOTO
  Goes on from a label in the command procedure.

  Format

    GOTO label

  A label is a name followed by a colon at the start of a procedure's
  line, $ NAME:. GOTO can go back or forward.
2 Parameters

 label

  The label to go to.
1 HELP
  Describes commands and other topics, from help libraries. At the
  "Topic?" prompt you can:

    Type the name of a command or topic, or the start of it.
    Type @name to look in a user help library: the list of them, if
    there are any, is after the topics.
    Type ? to see the last text again.
    Press Return to leave HELP, or to go back to the main library from
    another.

  At "Subtopic?", Return goes back up a level. CTRL/Z ends HELP.

  Format

    HELP [@library] [topic [subtopic ...]]
2 Parameters

 @library

  A user help library, by the start of its name, or a file, in SYS$HELP:
  with type .HLB unless you say otherwise. The topics after it are its.

 topic

  What to describe, followed by subtopics to go down to. A topic the
  library has not is looked for in the main library, then in the user
  libraries, and the library that has it is used until you press Return
  at its "@name Topic?".
2 Qualifiers
/INSTRUCTIONS
  /INSTRUCTIONS (default)
  /NOINSTRUCTIONS

  With no topic, writes this text before the list of topics.
/LIBLIST
  /LIBLIST (default)
  /NOLIBLIST

  After the list of topics, writes the user libraries that are there.
/LIBRARY
  /LIBRARY=file-spec

  The main help library, read instead of HELPLIB, in SYS$HELP: with type
  .HLB unless you say otherwise.
/OUTPUT
  /OUTPUT=file-spec
  /NOOUTPUT

  Writes the help to the file, HELP.LIS by default, instead of the
  terminal, and doesn't prompt unless you give /PROMPT. /NOOUTPUT writes
  it nowhere.
/PAGE
  /PAGE (default)
  /NOPAGE

  On a screen, clears it before each topic and stops when it is full,
  at "Press RETURN to continue ...". Typing a topic there goes to it,
  and ? stops the text.
/PROMPT
  /PROMPT (default)
  /NOPROMPT

  /NOPROMPT writes the help asked for and ends, without asking for more.
/USERLIBRARY
  /USERLIBRARY[=(level[,...])]
  /NOUSERLIBRARY

  Where the user help libraries are looked for: the libraries the
  logical names HLP$LIBRARY, HLP$LIBRARY_1, HLP$LIBRARY_2 and on name.

    PROCESS   in the process's logical name table
    GROUP     in the group's (vaxpunk has no group table)
    SYSTEM    in the system's
    ALL       in all of them, the default
    NONE      nowhere, as /NOUSERLIBRARY
2 Examples

  $ HELP COPY /LOG
  $ HELP/NOPROMPT SHOW LOGICAL
  $ HELP/LIBRARY=PHONEHELP
  $ DEFINE HLP$LIBRARY SYS$HELP:EDTHELP
  $ HELP @EDTHELP KEYPAD
  $ HELP/OUTPUT=COPY.TXT COPY
1 IF
  Does a command, or a block of them, only if an expression is true.

  Format

    IF expression THEN [$] command

    IF expression
    $ THEN
    $   command
    $   ...
    $ ELSE
    $   command
    $   ...
    $ ENDIF

  An expression is true when its value is odd. In the block form, THEN
  starts the commands done when it is true, ELSE those done when it is
  false, and ENDIF ends the block. ELSE and its commands can be left
  out, and a command can follow THEN or ELSE on the same line.
2 Parameters

 expression

  See the topic Expressions.

 command

  Any DCL command.
2 Examples

  $ IF P1 .EQS. "" THEN EXIT
  $ IF F$SEARCH("LOGIN.COM") .NES. ""
  $ THEN
  $   WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "There is a LOGIN.COM"
  $ ELSE
  $   WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "No LOGIN.COM"
  $ ENDIF
1 INITIALIZE
  Writes an empty Files-11 ODS-2 volume on a disk, which MOUNT then
  mounts. Whatever was on the disk is lost.

  Format

    INITIALIZE device-name volume-label
2 Parameters

 device-name

  The disk, which must not be mounted: DKB0:, the data disk, or MDA0:,
  the ramdisk.

 volume-label

  The volume's label, up to 12 characters.
2 Qualifiers
/OWNER_UIC
  /OWNER_UIC=uic

  Makes the volume that UIC's instead of yours.
/PROTECTION
  /PROTECTION=(code[,...])

  The volume's protection. See Specify Protection.
2 Examples

  $ INITIALIZE DKB0: DATA
  $ MOUNT DKB0: DATA
1 INQUIRE
  Asks the terminal for a symbol's value.

  Format

    INQUIRE symbol-name [prompt-string]

  It writes the prompt on a new line, followed by a colon and a space,
  and reads a line. Each 'name' in the line becomes the symbol's value,
  then the line is made a string as := makes one: in capitals, without
  the blanks at its ends and with one blank between words, but for the
  text in quotes, which is kept as it is, without the quotes.
2 Parameters

 symbol-name

  The symbol to set.

 prompt-string

  The prompt, the symbol's name if there is none. It is in capitals
  unless it is in quotes.
2 Qualifiers
/GLOBAL
  /GLOBAL

  Makes the symbol global.
/LOCAL
  /LOCAL (default)

  Makes the symbol local to the procedure.
/PUNCTUATION
  /PUNCTUATION (default)
  /NOPUNCTUATION

  /NOPUNCTUATION leaves out the colon and the space after the prompt.
2 Examples

  $ INQUIRE CHECK "Enter Y[ES] to continue"
  $ IF .NOT. CHECK THEN EXIT
1 Lexicals
  Lexical functions return information about strings, symbols, files,
  devices, processes and the system. Use one in an expression:

    F$name(argument[,...])

  Each argument is an expression of its own. The parentheses are
  needed even when there are no arguments.
2 F$EDIT
  Returns a string edited.

    F$EDIT(string, edit-list)

  The edits, separated by commas in one string: COLLAPSE takes out
  every blank and tab, COMPRESS makes each run of them one blank, TRIM
  takes them off both ends, UPCASE and LOWERCASE change the letters, and
  UNCOMMENT drops an exclamation mark and what follows. Text in quotes
  is left alone.
2 F$ELEMENT
  Returns one element of a list.

    F$ELEMENT(n, delimiter, string)

  The elements are the parts of string the delimiter separates, the
  first numbered 0. Past the last element it returns the delimiter.
2 F$ENVIRONMENT
  Returns information about DCL as it runs.

    F$ENVIRONMENT(item)

  The items: DEPTH, the procedure's depth; MAX_DEPTH; DEFAULT, the
  default device and directory; PROCEDURE, the procedure's file;
  ON_SEVERITY and ON_CONTROL_Y, what ON says; VERIFY_PROCEDURE and
  VERIFY_IMAGE; INTERACTIVE; and PROMPT.
2 F$EXTRACT
  Returns part of a string.

    F$EXTRACT(start, length, string)

  Returns length characters from start, the first numbered 0, or as
  many as there are.
2 F$FAO
  Returns a string formatted as the $FAO system service formats one.

    F$FAO(control-string[, argument[,...]])

  The directives it takes: !AS a string; !UB, !UW, !UL unsigned
  decimal; !SB, !SW, !SL signed; !ZB, !ZW, !ZL decimal with leading
  zeros; !XB, !XW, !XL hexadecimal; !OB, !OW, !OL octal; !%S an S when
  the last number wasn't 1; !/ a new line; !_ a tab; !! an exclamation
  mark; !n*c n copies of c. A width goes after the !, as !10AS.
2 F$GETDVI
  Returns an item of information about a device.

    F$GETDVI(device-name, item)

  The items: DEVNAM, VOLNAM, DEVCHAR, DEVCLASS, DEVTYPE, DEVBUFSIZ,
  DEVDEPEND, DEVDEPEND2, DEVDEPEND3, UNIT, ERRCNT, REFCNT, FREEBLOCKS,
  MOUNTCNT, CLUSTER, STS, AVL, MNT and SWL, and EXISTS, TRUE if there is
  such a device.
2 F$GETJPI
  Returns an item of information about a process.

    F$GETJPI(pid, item)

  The pid is the process's identification in hexadecimal, or "" for
  your own. The items: PID, OWNER, PRCNAM, IMAGNAME, UIC, GRP, MEM, PRI,
  PRIB, STATE, STS, CURPRIV, PROCPRIV, AUTHPRIV, TERMINAL and MODE.
2 F$GETSYI
  Returns an item of information about the system.

    F$GETSYI(item)

  The items: VERSION, HW_NAME, BOOTTIME, MAXPROCESSCNT, QUANTUM,
  ACTIVECPU_CNT, AVAILCPU_CNT, PRIMARY_CPUID, PAGE_SIZE, MEMSIZE,
  FREE_PAGES, NPAGED_TOTAL, NPAGED_FREE, NPAGED_INUSE, NPAGED_LARGEST,
  and STARTUP_P1 to STARTUP_P8.
2 F$INTEGER
  Returns its argument as an integer.

    F$INTEGER(expression)
2 F$LENGTH
  Returns the length of a string.

    F$LENGTH(string)
2 F$LOCATE
  Returns where a string is first found in another, counting from 0,
  or the other's length if it isn't there.

    F$LOCATE(substring, string)
2 F$MESSAGE
  Returns the message text of a status.

    F$MESSAGE(status-code)
2 F$MODE
  Returns how the process runs: INTERACTIVE when SYS$INPUT is a
  terminal, BATCH when it is a file, else OTHER.

    F$MODE()
2 F$PARSE
  Returns a file specification made whole, with the default's parts
  where it leaves them out, or one field of it.

    F$PARSE(file-spec[, default-spec[, related-spec[, field]]])

  The fields: NODE, DEVICE, DIRECTORY, NAME, TYPE and VERSION. It
  returns "" if the specification can't be parsed.
2 F$SEARCH
  Returns the next file a specification names, or "" when there are no
  more.

    F$SEARCH(file-spec[, stream-id])

  With a wildcard, each call with the same specification returns the
  next file. Each stream, an integer, searches on its own, so one
  search can run inside another.
2 F$STRING
  Returns its argument as a string.

    F$STRING(expression)
2 F$TIME
  Returns the date and time now, as dd-mmm-yyyy hh:mm:ss.cc.

    F$TIME()
2 F$TRNLNM
  Returns a logical name's equivalence string, or "" if it has none.

    F$TRNLNM(logical-name[, table[, index]])

  The table is LNM$FILE_DEV unless given; index picks one of a search
  list's names, the first numbered 0.
2 F$TYPE
  Returns what a symbol holds: INTEGER, STRING, or "" if there is no
  such symbol. A string of digits counts as an integer.

    F$TYPE(symbol-name)
2 F$VERIFY
  Returns 1 if SET VERIFY is on, else 0, and with an argument turns it
  on or off, as the argument is true or false.

    F$VERIFY([procedure-value])
1 Line_editing
  As you type a command line, these keys edit it:

    Delete          erases the character before the cursor
    Left, Right     move the cursor; also CTRL/D and CTRL/F
    CTRL/H          goes to the start of the line
    CTRL/E          goes to the end of the line
    CTRL/J          erases the word before the cursor
    CTRL/U          erases everything before the cursor
    CTRL/A          changes between insert and overstrike
    CTRL/R          writes the line again
    Up, CTRL/B      recall the line typed before, and before that
    Down            goes back down the recalled lines
    Return          ends the line
    CTRL/Z          ends the line, and the input

  CTRL/Y stops the image that runs, and DCL prompts; CONTINUE goes on
  with it. SET TERMINAL/NOLINE_EDITING turns the editing keys off, and
  /INSERT and /OVERSTRIKE choose the mode a line starts in.
1 LOGOUT
  Ends your session: says you logged out, and ends the process.

  Format

    LOGOUT
1 MCR
  Runs an image from SYS$SYSTEM and hands it the rest of the line.

  Format

    MCR image-name [command]

  The image, SYS$SYSTEM:image-name.EXE, reads the command with
  LIB$GET_FOREIGN.
1 MOUNT
  Mounts a disk's volume, so its files can be named. Without /SYSTEM
  the volume is private: the disk is allocated to your job, which alone
  may use it, its logical names are in your process table, and it is
  dismounted when you log out.

  Format

    MOUNT device-name volume-label [logical-name]
2 Parameters

 device-name

  The disk to mount.

 volume-label

  The label the volume must have. With /OVERRIDE=IDENTIFICATION, any
  or none.

 logical-name

  A logical name for the disk, besides DISK$volume-label, which MOUNT
  always defines. DISMOUNT deletes both.
2 Qualifiers
/OVERRIDE
  /OVERRIDE=IDENTIFICATION

  Mounts the volume whatever its label. Not with /SYSTEM.
/OWNER_UIC
  /OWNER_UIC=uic

  The owner the volume has while it is mounted, in place of its own.
/PROTECTION
  /PROTECTION=(code[,...])

  The protection the volume has while it is mounted. See Specify
  Protection.
/SYSTEM
  /SYSTEM

  Mounts the volume for every process, its logical names in the system
  table. Requires SYSNAM.
/WRITE
  /WRITE (default)
  /NOWRITE

  /NOWRITE mounts the volume read only.
2 Examples

  $ MOUNT/SYSTEM DKB0: DATA
  $ MOUNT/OVERRIDE=IDENTIFICATION DKB0:
1 ON
  Says what a command procedure does when a command fails, or when you
  press CTRL/Y.

  Format

    ON condition THEN [$] command

  The conditions: WARNING, ERROR and SEVERE_ERROR, a command whose
  status is that bad or worse; and CONTROL_Y. Without an ON a procedure
  ends at an error or a fatal error. An ON for an error is done once,
  and the procedure is back on ON ERROR THEN EXIT after it. SET NOON
  turns the checks off.
2 Examples

  $ ON ERROR THEN GOTO CLEANUP
  $ ON CONTROL_Y THEN EXIT
1 OPEN
  Opens a file for READ and WRITE, under a logical name.

  Format

    OPEN logical-name file-spec

  The file stays open until CLOSE or the end of the process. It can be
  sequential, relative or indexed.
2 Parameters

 logical-name

  The name READ, WRITE and CLOSE will know the file by.

 file-spec

  The file to open.
2 Qualifiers
/APPEND
  /APPEND

  Opens an existing file to write records after its last one.
/ERROR
  /ERROR=label

  Goes to the label if the file can't be opened.
/READ
  /READ (default)

  Opens the file for reading.
/SHARE
  /SHARE[=READ|WRITE]

  Taken for VMS's procedures; it changes nothing.
/WRITE
  /WRITE

  Alone, makes a new sequential file to write. With /READ, opens an
  existing file for both.
2 Examples

  $ OPEN/WRITE OUT LIST.TXT
  $ WRITE OUT "First line"
  $ CLOSE OUT
1 PHONE
  Talks with another user of the system, a character at a time, each
  in a window of the screen.

  Format

    PHONE [phone-command]

  Type a command after the switch hook, %, or straight away when no one
  is in the call. PHONE's HELP describes its commands.
2 Parameters

 phone-command

  A command PHONE does first, as DIAL user.
2 Qualifiers
/SWITCH_HOOK
  /SWITCH_HOOK="c"

  The character that starts a command, % by default.
/VIEWPORT_SIZE
  /VIEWPORT_SIZE=n

  The most lines a person's window has, 10 by default.
2 Examples

  $ PHONE JOE
1 PIPE
  Does several commands on one line.

  Format

    PIPE command1 ; command2
    PIPE command1 && command2
    PIPE command1 || command2

  ; does the next command anyway, && only if the one before succeeded,
  || only if it failed. They are taken from left to right. A separator
  in quotes is part of the command.
2 Examples

  $ PIPE SET DEFAULT [WORK] && DIRECTORY
  $ PIPE TYPE NOTES.TXT || WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "No notes"
1 PURGE
  Deletes all but the highest version of each file, or the highest n
  with /KEEP=n.

  Format

    PURGE [file-spec]
2 Parameters

 file-spec

  The files to purge, *.* in the default directory if it is left out.
  It gives no version number. Wildcards are allowed.
2 Qualifiers
/KEEP
  /KEEP=n

  How many versions of each file to keep, 1 if it is left out.
/LOG
  /LOG
  /NOLOG (default)

  Says which files were deleted, and how many.
2 Examples

  $ PURGE
  $ PURGE/KEEP=2/LOG [WORK]*.TXT
1 READ
  Reads a record of a file OPEN opened into a symbol.

  Format

    READ logical-name symbol-name

  It reads the next record, or with /KEY the one with that key. The
  symbol is local. READ SYS$INPUT reads a procedure's next data line,
  or a line from the terminal.
2 Parameters

 logical-name

  The name OPEN gave the file, or SYS$INPUT or SYS$COMMAND.

 symbol-name

  The symbol the record goes in.
2 Qualifiers
/DELETE
  /DELETE

  Deletes the record after reading it, in an indexed file.
/END_OF_FILE
  /END_OF_FILE=label

  Goes to the label when there are no more records.
/ERROR
  /ERROR=label

  Goes to the label if the read fails.
/INDEX
  /INDEX=n

  The key /KEY looks along, 0 the primary key.
/KEY
  /KEY=string

  Reads the record with this key. The next READ without /KEY reads on
  from it along the same key.
/MATCH
  /MATCH=option

  How /KEY matches: EQ, the default, the first record whose key starts
  with the string; GE, the first equal or greater; GT, the first greater.
/PROMPT
  /PROMPT=string

  The prompt READ SYS$INPUT writes on the terminal.
2 Examples

  $ READ/PROMPT="Name: " SYS$INPUT NAME
  $ LOOP:
  $ READ/END_OF_FILE=DONE IN LINE
  $ WRITE SYS$OUTPUT LINE
  $ GOTO LOOP
  $ DONE:
1 REQUEST
  Writes a message on the operators' terminals, through OPCOM, after
  the name of your terminal.

  Format

    REQUEST message-text
2 Parameters

 message-text

  The message. Put it in quotation marks if it has more than one word,
  or lower case letters to keep.
2 Qualifiers
/TO
  /TO=(operator[,...])

  The operators the message is for: CARDS, CENTRAL, CLUSTER, DEVICES,
  DISKS, LICENSE, NETWORK, OPER1 to OPER12, PRINTER, SECURITY or TAPES.
  Without it, it is for all of them.
2 Examples

  $ REQUEST "Please mount the backup disk on DKB0:"
  $ REQUEST/TO=DISKS "DKB0: is full"
1 RUN
  Runs an image in the process, or in a new process: a subprocess of
  yours, or with /DETACHED or /UIC a detached process.

  Format

    RUN file-spec

  The type is .EXE unless you give one. The image ends with a status,
  which becomes $STATUS.
2 Parameters

 file-spec

  The image to run.
2 Qualifiers
/DETACHED
  /DETACHED

  Runs the image in a new process, detached from yours, which goes on
  after you log out. Without it, and without /UIC, the new process is
  a subprocess of yours, which goes when your process does. Either way
  RUN says its identification. It has your username and default
  directory, and, unless the qualifiers below say otherwise, your UIC,
  base priority, permanent privileges and I/O quotas, no name, and no
  SYS$INPUT, SYS$OUTPUT or SYS$ERROR. Each of the qualifiers below runs
  the image in a new process too. Of the quotas, only those of
  /BUFFER_LIMIT, /IO_BUFFERED and /IO_DIRECT are kept yet, raised to
  1024, 4 and 4 if they are less.
/ERROR
  /ERROR=file-spec

  The new process's SYS$ERROR.
/INPUT
  /INPUT=file-spec

  The new process's SYS$INPUT. Its DCL, if it runs SYS$SYSTEM:DCL, runs
  the file as a command procedure, and logs out at its end.
/MAILBOX
  /MAILBOX=unit

  The unit number of a mailbox that gets the new process's termination
  message when it is deleted.
/OUTPUT
  /OUTPUT=file-spec

  The new process's SYS$OUTPUT.
/PRIORITY
  /PRIORITY=n

  The new process's base priority, 0 to 31; yours if it is left out.
/PRIVILEGES
  /PRIVILEGES=(privilege[,...])

  The new process's privileges: your permanent ones, or none if the list
  has NOSAME, with each privilege named added and each NOprivilege taken
  away; ALL is every one. It gets only those you have, unless you have
  SETPRV.
/PROCESS_NAME
  /PROCESS_NAME=name

  The new process's name, up to 15 characters.
/UIC
  /UIC=[group,member]

  The new process's UIC, in octal, which makes it a detached process;
  one other than yours takes the DETACH privilege.
/AST_LIMIT
  /AST_LIMIT=n

  The new process's quota of ASTs it may have queued, ASTLM.
/BUFFER_LIMIT
  /BUFFER_LIMIT=n

  The new process's quota of bytes of buffered I/O it may have at once, BYTLM.
/ENQUEUE_LIMIT
  /ENQUEUE_LIMIT=n

  The new process's quota of locks it may hold, ENQLM.
/EXTENT
  /EXTENT=n

  The new process's quota of pages its working set may grow to, WSEXTENT.
/FILE_LIMIT
  /FILE_LIMIT=n

  The new process's quota of files it may have open, FILLM.
/IO_BUFFERED
  /IO_BUFFERED=n

  The new process's quota of buffered I/O requests it may have at once, BIOLM.
/IO_DIRECT
  /IO_DIRECT=n

  The new process's quota of direct I/O requests it may have at once, DIOLM.
/JOB_TABLE_QUOTA
  /JOB_TABLE_QUOTA=n

  The new process's quota of bytes its job logical name table may hold, JTQUOTA.
/MAXIMUM_WORKING_SET
  /MAXIMUM_WORKING_SET=n

  The new process's quota of pages of its working set quota, WSQUOTA.
/PAGE_FILE
  /PAGE_FILE=n

  The new process's quota of pages of the paging file it may use, PGFLQUOTA.
/QUEUE_LIMIT
  /QUEUE_LIMIT=n

  The new process's quota of timer queue entries it may have, TQELM.
/SUBPROCESS_LIMIT
  /SUBPROCESS_LIMIT=n

  The new process's quota of subprocesses it may have, PRCLM.
/WORKING_SET
  /WORKING_SET=n

  The new process's quota of pages of its default working set, WSDEFAULT.
2 Examples

  $ RUN/DETACHED/PRIORITY=6/PROCESS_NAME=OPCOM SYS$SYSTEM:OPCOM
1 SET
  Sets characteristics of the process, the terminal, files,
  directories, volumes and DCL.

  Format

    SET option
2 COMMAND
  Adds the verbs a command definition file defines to your process's
  commands.

    SET COMMAND file-spec

  The file's type is .CLD unless you give one.
2 CONTROL
  Turns CTRL/Y, and CTRL/C where no image takes it, on or off.

    SET CONTROL[=Y]
    SET NOCONTROL[=Y]
2 DEFAULT
  Sets the default device and directory, where files are looked for
  when their specification doesn't say.

    SET DEFAULT [device:][directory]

  A logical name will do, with or without its colon. [-] goes up a
  level.
3 Examples

  $ SET DEFAULT DKB0:[PROJECT]
  $ SET DEFAULT [-]
2 DIRECTORY
  Sets a directory's default version limit: the most versions a name
  made in it later may have.

    SET DIRECTORY/VERSION_LIMIT[=n] directory-spec
3 Qualifiers
/VERSION_LIMIT
  /VERSION_LIMIT[=n]

  The limit, up to 32767; 0, or no value, is none. Names already in
  the directory keep theirs.
3 Examples

  $ SET DIRECTORY/VERSION_LIMIT=3 [PROJECT]
2 FILE
  Changes a file's owner, record attributes, expiration date or version
  limit.

    SET FILE file-spec
3 Qualifiers
/ATTRIBUTE
  /ATTRIBUTE=(keyword:value[,...])

  Changes the file's record attributes: BKS, DEQ, EBK, FFB, FSZ, GBC,
  LRL, MRS and VRS take a number; ORG takes SEQ, REL or IDX; RFM takes
  UDF, FIX, VAR, VFC, STM, STMLF or STMCR; RAT takes NONE, FTN, CR, PRN,
  BLK or MSB, the first RAT replacing them, any more adding to them.
/EXPIRATION_DATE
  /EXPIRATION_DATE=time
  /NOEXPIRATION_DATE

  When the file expires, or none. A time is dd-mmm-yyyy:hh:mm:ss.cc,
  the parts left out today's, or TODAY, TOMORROW or YESTERDAY.
/OWNER_UIC
  /OWNER_UIC=uic

  The file's new owner. See Specify UIC.
/VERSION_LIMIT
  /VERSION_LIMIT[=n]

  The most versions the file's name may have in its directory, up to
  32767; 0, or no value, is none. A version made past it deletes the
  lowest.
3 Examples

  $ SET FILE/ATTRIBUTE=(RFM:FIX,MRS:80,RAT:NONE) DATA.BIN
  $ SET FILE/VERSION_LIMIT=2 LOGIN.COM
2 HOST
  Logs in on another system over the network, with Telnet.

    SET HOST node

  The node is an address or a host's name. CTRL/] gives the TELNET>
  prompt: CONNECT or Return goes back, CLOSE or EXIT ends the session.
  See TELNET.
2 ON
  Turns a procedure's error checks on or off.

    SET ON
    SET NOON

  With SET NOON a procedure goes on after a failed command. See ON.
2 PASSWORD
  Changes your password. It asks for the old one, then the new one
  twice, without echoing them.

    SET PASSWORD
2 PROCESS
  Enables or disables your process's privileges.

    SET PROCESS/PRIVILEGES=(privilege[,...])
3 Qualifiers
/PRIVILEGES
  /PRIVILEGES=(privilege[,...])

  Each privilege named is enabled, and each named NOprivilege disabled.
  ALL and NOALL name every one. You only get those you are authorized
  for.
2 PROTECTION
  Changes a file's protection.

    SET PROTECTION=(code[,...]) file-spec

  See Specify Protection.
3 Examples

  $ SET PROTECTION=(S:RWED,O:RWED,G:RE,W) REPORT.TXT
2 VOLUME
  Changes a mounted volume's label, owner and protection, on the
  volume. It takes the CMKRNL privilege.

    SET VOLUME device-name[:]
3 Qualifiers
/FILE_PROTECTION
  /FILE_PROTECTION=(code[,...])

  The volume's default file protection. See Specify Protection.
/LABEL
  /LABEL=volume-label

  The volume's new label, 1 to 12 characters.
/OWNER_UIC
  /OWNER_UIC=uic

  The volume's new owner. See Specify UIC.
/PROTECTION
  /PROTECTION=(code[,...])

  The volume's protection: the categories named get that access, the
  others keep theirs. See Specify Protection.
2 TERMINAL
  Sets your terminal's characteristics.

    SET TERMINAL [device-name]
3 Qualifiers
/BROADCAST
  /BROADCAST
  /NOBROADCAST

  Whether messages, such as a PHONE call's ring, are written on the
  terminal.
/COLOR
  /COLOR
  /NOCOLOR

  Whether the terminal takes ANSI colour escape sequences.
/DEVICE_TYPE
  /DEVICE_TYPE=terminal-type

  Says what the terminal is, and gives it that type's characteristics:
  UNKNOWN, VT52, VT100, VT102, VT200, VT300, VT400 or VT500.
/ECHO
  /ECHO
  /NOECHO

  Whether what you type is echoed.
/EIGHTBIT
  /EIGHTBIT
  /NOEIGHTBIT

  Whether the terminal takes 8-bit characters.
/ESCAPE
  /ESCAPE
  /NOESCAPE

  Whether escape sequences end a read.
/INQUIRE
  /INQUIRE

  Asks the terminal what it is, and gives it that type's
  characteristics, as /DEVICE_TYPE does, then asks a DEC terminal its
  size, its page and width. A terminal that doesn't answer within 3
  seconds keeps its characteristics: %SET-I-UNKTERM.
/INSERT
  /INSERT

  Line editing starts each line in insert mode.
/LINE_EDITING
  /LINE_EDITING
  /NOLINE_EDITING

  Whether the line editing keys work. See Line_editing.
/LOWERCASE
  /LOWERCASE
  /NOLOWERCASE

  Whether lowercase letters are kept, or made capitals.
/OVERSTRIKE
  /OVERSTRIKE

  Line editing starts each line in overstrike mode.
/PAGE
  /PAGE=n

  The terminal's lines, up to 255.
/PERMANENT
  /PERMANENT

  Sets the permanent characteristics, and the current ones: the
  terminal goes back to them once no process has it. Takes LOG_IO or
  PHY_IO.
/PASTHRU
  /PASTHRU
  /NOPASTHRU

  Passes every character to the program, CTRL/Y and CTRL/C too.
/TAB
  /TAB
  /NOTAB

  Whether the terminal handles tabs itself.
/SPEED
  /SPEED=rate
  /SPEED=(input-rate,output-rate)

  The speeds at which the terminal receives and sends, in baud: 50, 75,
  110, 134, 150, 300, 600, 1200, 1800, 2000, 2400, 3600, 4800, 7200,
  9600, 19200, 38400, 57600, 76800 or 115200.
/WIDTH
  /WIDTH=n

  The terminal's columns, from 1 to 511.
/WRAP
  /WRAP
  /NOWRAP

  Whether a line longer than the width goes on to the next.
2 VERIFY
  Writes a procedure's commands as they are done, or stops.

    SET VERIFY
    SET NOVERIFY
1 SHOW
  Shows information about the process, the system, DCL and the
  devices.

  Format

    SHOW option
2 CPU
  Shows the machine and its CPUs.
3 Qualifiers
/BRIEF
  /BRIEF

  Shows each CPU and the process it runs.
/FULL
  /FULL

  Shows the page size as well.
2 DEFAULT
  Shows the default device and directory. A search list's names follow,
  one a line.
2 DEVICES
  Lists the devices.

    SHOW DEVICES [device-name]

  With a name, the devices whose names start with it.
3 Qualifiers
/FULL
  /FULL

  Describes each device in a paragraph: what it is, its counts and its
  volume's.
/MOUNTED
  /MOUNTED

  Lists only the devices with a volume mounted.
2 ERROR
  Lists the devices that have had errors, and how many.
3 Qualifiers
/FULL
  /FULL

  Lists every device, with or without errors.
2 LOGICAL
  Shows a logical name's equivalence names, or all of them.

    SHOW LOGICAL [logical-name]

  With no name, or *, it lists every logical name.
3 Qualifiers
/PROCESS
  /PROCESS

  Looks only in the process's table.
/SYSTEM
  /SYSTEM

  Looks only in the system's table.
/TABLE
  /TABLE=table-name

  Looks only in that table.
2 MEMORY
  Shows the physical memory, the process slots and nonpaged pool.
3 Qualifiers
/ALL
  /ALL

  Shows them all. This is the default.
/PHYSICAL_PAGES
  /PHYSICAL_PAGES

  Shows the physical pages.
/POOL
  /POOL

  Shows nonpaged pool.
/SLOTS
  /SLOTS

  Shows the process slots.
2 PROCESS
  Shows the date, your process's name, UIC, terminal, priority and
  image.
3 Qualifiers
/PRIVILEGES
  /PRIVILEGES

  Shows your authorized and current privileges as well.
2 QUOTA
  Shows your disk quota. No volume has disk quotas, and it says so.
3 Qualifiers
/DISK
  /DISK[=device-name]

  The disk to ask about, SYS$DISK by default.
/USER
  /USER=uic

  The user to ask about.
2 SYMBOL
  Shows a symbol's value.

    SHOW SYMBOL symbol-name
    SHOW SYMBOL/ALL

  It shows the nearest symbol of that name: local, then global.
3 Qualifiers
/ALL
  /ALL

  Shows every symbol of the level, local unless /GLOBAL.
/GLOBAL
  /GLOBAL

  Shows the global symbol.
/LOCAL
  /LOCAL

  Shows the local symbol.
2 SYSTEM
  Shows the version, the date and how long the system has been up,
  then a line for each process: its PID, name, state, priority and
  image.
2 TERMINAL
  Shows a terminal's characteristics, yours unless you name another.

    SHOW TERMINAL [device-name]
3 Qualifiers
/PERMANENT
  /PERMANENT

  Shows the permanent characteristics, which the terminal goes back to
  once no process has it. Takes LOG_IO or PHY_IO.
2 USERS
  Lists the users logged in at terminals, and how many processes each
  has.

    SHOW USERS [username]

  With a username, only the users whose names start with it.
3 Qualifiers
/FULL
  /FULL

  Lists each process and its terminal.
1 Specify
  How to write the values commands take.
2 File_spec
  A file specification names a file:

    device:[directory]name.type;version

  The device is a disk, as DKA0:, or a logical name for one; the
  directory is [name], with subdirectories as [name.sub]; the name and
  type are up to 39 characters each; the version is a number. What you
  leave out comes from the default device and directory, SET DEFAULT's,
  and the newest version.

  A logical name may stand for the device, or for the whole
  specification. A search list, a logical name with more than one
  equivalence, is looked through in order.

  Where a command takes wildcards, * matches any characters and % one,
  and in the directory [*] is any directory, [dir...] dir and every
  directory under it, and [...] the default one and those under it.
2 Protection
  A protection code says what each category of user may do with a file
  or volume:

    (category:access[,...])

  The categories are SYSTEM, OWNER, GROUP and WORLD, or their first
  letters. The access is any of R, read, W, write, E, execute, and D,
  delete, after a colon or an equals sign; a category with no letters
  gets no access. A category you leave out keeps what it had with SET
  PROTECTION, and gets all with INITIALIZE and MOUNT.
3 Example

  (S:RWED,O:RWED,G:RE,W)
2 UIC
  A user identification code, a UIC, is a group and a member number, in
  octal, in brackets:

    [group,member]
1 STOP
  Deletes a process, or ends the image CTRL/Y stopped.

  Format

    STOP [process-name]
    STOP/IDENTIFICATION=pid

  With no name or /IDENTIFICATION, ends the image stopped by CTRL/Y,
  without its exit handlers, and every command procedure.
2 Parameters

 process-name

  The name of the process to delete.
2 Qualifiers
/IDENTIFICATION
  /IDENTIFICATION=pid

  The process to delete, by its identification in hexadecimal.
1 Symbol_Assign
  A symbol is a name for an integer or a string. Make one with = or ==,
  := or :==; see the topics = and :=. Show one with SHOW SYMBOL, and
  delete it with DELETE/SYMBOL.

  Local symbols belong to the command procedure that made them, and the
  procedures it calls see them. Global symbols are everyone's. DCL sets
  $STATUS to each command's status and $SEVERITY to its severity.

  A symbol at the start of a command stands for its value: HOME ==
  "SET DEFAULT SYS$LOGIN" makes HOME a command.
1 TCPIP
  TCP/IP Services' management commands.

  Format

    TCPIP [command]

  With no command it prompts TCPIP> for them until EXIT or CTRL/Z. The
  commands are PING, SET and SHOW HOST, INTERFACE, NAME_SERVICE and
  ROUTE, SET CONFIGURATION INTERFACE, SET NOHOST and START
  COMMUNICATION; HELP at TCPIP> describes them.
2 Examples

  $ TCPIP SHOW INTERFACE
  $ TCPIP PING 10.0.2.2
1 TELNET
  Logs in on another system with Telnet, or connects to a TCP port and
  talks to what listens there a line at a time.

  Format

    TELNET node [port]
    TELNET node /PORT=n

  The node is an address or a host's name. CTRL/] gives the TELNET>
  prompt: CONNECT or Return goes back to the session, CLOSE, EXIT or
  QUIT ends it.
2 Parameters

 node

  The system to connect to.

 port

  The TCP port, 23, Telnet's, by default.
2 Qualifiers
/PORT
  /PORT=n

  The TCP port to connect to.
1 TYPE
  Writes a file's records on the terminal, a line each.

  Format

    TYPE file-spec
2 Parameters

 file-spec

  The file to type.
2 Qualifiers
/HEAD
  /HEAD[=n]

  Types only the first n lines, 10 if you don't say.
/PAGE
  /PAGE[=keyword]
  /NOPAGE (default)

  On a video terminal, writes a screen at a time and asks "Press
  RETURN to continue" on its last line. RETURN shows more, CTRL/Z
  stops. The keywords:

  CLEAR_SCREEN  Clears the screen before each page.
  SCROLL        Scrolls on, the last line written staying at the
                top (the default).
  SAVE[=n]      As SCROLL here: there are no keys to go back.
/TAIL
  /TAIL[=n]

  Types only the last n lines, 10 if you don't say.
1 WRITE
  Writes a record to a file OPEN opened, or a line on SYS$OUTPUT.

  Format

    WRITE logical-name expression[,...]

  The expressions' values are joined into one record.
2 Parameters

 logical-name

  The name OPEN gave the file, or SYS$OUTPUT or SYS$ERROR.

 expression[,...]

  What to write. See Expressions.
2 Qualifiers
/ERROR
  /ERROR=label

  Goes to the label if the write fails.
/UPDATE
  /UPDATE

  Rewrites the record READ read last, in place.
2 Examples

  $ WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "X is ", X
